-- title:
--   A predicate avoiding every maximal ideal yields a finite unit-ideal cover
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring and let $P : B \to \mathrm{Prop}$ be an arbitrary predicate on the elements of $B$, subject to no stability assumption. Suppose that for every ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $B$ that is maximal there exists an element $s \in B$ with $s \notin \mathfrak m$ and $P(s)$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a natural number $n$ and a family $g : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to B$ such that the ideal generated by the range of $g$ is the whole of $B$ (the top ideal) and $P(g_i)$ holds for every index $i$. Thus finitely many elements each satisfying $P$ generate the unit ideal; the number $n$ is produced by the statement and is not bounded in any way, and in the zero ring the empty family ($n = 0$) is admissible.
--
--   This is the standard Zariski-compactness mechanism: a condition holding on a basic open neighbourhood of every closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} B$ holds on a finite cover by basic opens, packaged here in the form 'finitely many elements satisfying $P$ generate the unit ideal'. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_cover_free_and_span_range_eq_map_of_subgroup_ideal`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_cover_free_and_span_range_eq_map_of_subgroup_ideal), to pass from a pointwise local statement to a finite covering family.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_exists_span_range_eq_top_of_forall_isMaximal_exists_notMem.lean

